# The carry-on rules over one item

> Module AirlineCarryOn

> Using AirlineWorld as W
> Using Airline as A

A concolic harness, not part of the port: it adds no rule, it only puts one of
the port's existing rules behind a scope whose inputs CUTECat can encode.

`W.Item` is the largest piece of the airline world model that holds no list ---
a kind and four integers --- and the carry-on section is the part of the
statute that reads it directly.  So this scope is exactly the branching of
`carry_on_fits` and `item_exempt`: eight constructors, three componentwise
dimension tests for an ordinary bag, and one sum test for a garment bag.
CUTECat should find every one of them, and nothing here can make it drop a path.

Compare `AirlineTrip.catala_en`, which reaches for the fee tables and cannot.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope CarryOn:
  input it content W.Item
  ## "cannot exceed 22 x 14 x 9 inches", or 51 inches summed for a soft-sided
  ## garment bag.
  output fits content boolean
  ## "These don't count as your personal item or carry-on" --- the kinds the
  ## statute puts outside the allowance altogether.
  output exempt content boolean
  ## The "Weight and Size" total, which the checked-bag rules compare to 62.
  output size content integer
```

```catala
scope CarryOn:
  definition fits equals A.carry_on_fits of it
  definition exempt equals A.item_exempt of it
  definition size equals W.size_sum of it
```
